{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929","id":"msg_01As72Z3jYVKM5EdkyzBrBYd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Great! I can see that both `_llm_alias_resolution` (line 1118) and `_llm_alias_resolution_pairwise` (line 991) call `_validate_merge`. The problem is that the validation is happening AFTER the LLM suggests the merge. But based on the evaluation state, we have 58 characters initially grouped, which is less than 60, so it would use the global LLM alias resolution.\n\nNow, let me understand what's happening. The fix added in attempt 3 checks for family members in `_validate_merge` (lines 1522-1530), but it seems like this validation isn't preventing the merge. Let me add some debug logging to understand what's happening.\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the code, I notice that the validation at lines 1522-1530 should reject \"George Wilson\" + \"Myrtle Wilson\" because:\n- first1=\"George\", last1=\"Wilson\"\n- first2=\"Myrtle\", last2=\"Wilson\"\n- They have the same last name but different first names\n\nSo the validation SHOULD be triggering and returning `False, 0.05`. But the merge is still happening. This suggests either:\n1. The validation is not being called for this pair\n2. The validation is being called but something else is merging them\n3. There's a bug in the validation logic\n\nLet me check if there's a \"Wilson\" (single word) that's being merged with both \"George Wilson\" and \"Myrtle Wilson\". Looking back at the output, I see the canonical name is \"Wilson\" with aliases including both \"George Wilson\" and \"Myrtle Wilson\". This suggests \"Wilson\" is being used as the canonical name.\n\nSo the merges might be:\n1. \"Wilson\" + \"George Wilson\" → canonical: \"Wilson\", alias: \"George Wilson\"\n2. \"Wilson\" + \"Myrtle Wilson\" → canonical: \"Wilson\", alias: \"Myrtle Wilson\"\n\nLet me check if the validation logic covers this case. Looking at lines 1532-1568, there IS