{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929","id":"msg_01BB7LF8gXy83JHnmrpmXM8b","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Looking at the validation logic at lines 1532-1568, the code checks:\n- If one name is a single word and matches a last name\n- Count how many DIFFERENT first names exist with this last name\n- If multiple people have this last name (len(different_first_names) > 1), reject the merge\n\nThe issue is at line 1562: the check is `if len(different_first_names) > 1`. This means it only rejects if there are at least TWO different first names. But at the time \"Wilson\" is being merged with \"George Wilson\", there might only be ONE first name detected so far (George), so the check doesn't trigger!\n\nThen later, when \"Wilson\" (which already includes \"George Wilson\" as an alias) is being merged with \"Myrtle Wilson\", the merge might go through a different code path or the name_groups might not include the already-merged names.\n\nLet me look more closely at how this works. The key insight is that the LLM returns a list of suggested merges all at once in the global mode, and then `_validate_merge` is called for each suggested alias. But by that point, the name_groups still contains the original names, not the already-merged ones.\n\nActually, looking at line 1548: `for name in name_groups.keys()` - it's iterating over the ORIGINAL name_groups that were passed in, which should include all the original names including both \"George Wilson\" and \"Myrtle Wilson\". So the check should work...\n\nUnless... let me check the exact condition more carefully. At line 1558: `if name_last == single_word`. This checks if a multi-word name's last component equals the single word. So if we're validating \"Wilson\" + \"George Wilson\":\n- single_word = \"Wilson\"\n- We look for names where the last component is \"Wilson\"\n- We should find \"George Wilson\" (name_last=\"wilson\") and \"Myrtle Wilson\" (name_last=\"