nd a wreath for her mother’s grave, while Catherine reveals Myrtle married George believing he was a gentleman—only to bitterly regret it—until Myrtle defiantly shouts Daisy’s name, provoking Tom to violently break her nose with a single blow, triggering screams, bloodied towels, and frantic scrambling among the women, after which Nick, overwhelmed and detached, leaves with Mr. McKee, who sleeps through the violence and later invites him to lunch, ending the night exhausted in the cold waiting room of Pennsylvania Station, staring at the morning Tribune.\\\"\\n226:        \\\"George Wilson\\\",\\n227:        \\\"Myrtle Wilson\\\",\\n231:      \\\"summary\\\": \\\"On the hottest day of summer, the narrator arrives by train at the Buchanans’ house to find Gatsby waiting, having dismissed his staff and replaced them with Wolfsheim’s silent relatives; Daisy openly kisses Gatsby and declares her love, shocking Tom, who immediately begins questioning Gatsby’s background. The group departs for New York in two cars—Gatsby driving his own vehicle, Tom taking Daisy in the coupé—stopping at Wilson’s garage in the valley of ashes, where Wilson, visibly broken, reveals his plan to flee with Myrtle and offers to buy Tom’s old car, while Myrtle watches from the window, mistaking Jordan for Tom’s wife in a moment of jealous terror beneath the faded eyes of Doctor T. J. Eckleburg. At the Plaza Hotel, a volatile argument erupts in a stifling suite as Tom accuses Gatsby of bootlegging and lying about Oxford; Daisy reluctantly admits she once loved Tom but now loves Gatsby, yet refuses to say she never loved Tom, shattering Gatsby’s idealized vision. Tom orders Daisy and Gatsby to leave together in Gatsby’s car, ending the confrontation. Later, Myrtle Wilson rushes out into the dusk and is struck and killed by a speeding yellow car that flees the scene; Michaelis and others find her body mangled, with a torn left breast and split mouth. Tom and the narrator arrive at the garage to find Wilson hysterical, crying 'Oh, my God!' as Tom falsely reassures him the yellow car was not his. That night, Gatsby emerges from the shadows outside the Buchanan house in his pink suit, confessing to Nick that Daisy was driving the car that killed Myrtle but insisting he will take the blame; Nick finds Tom and Daisy calmly eating cold chicken together in the kitchen, their posture intimate and conspiratorial, while Gatsby stands vigil in the moonlight, refusing to leave, watching over nothing.\\\"\\n249:        \\\"George Wilson\\\",\\n251:        \\\"Myrtle Wilson\\\",\\n256:      \\\"summary\\\": \\\"The chapter unfolds across Long Island and New York City, beginning with a sleepless night and dawn at Gatsby’s mansion, where the narrator rushes to find Gatsby dejected after Daisy briefly appeared at her window before turning off the light. The two wander through the dusty, musty mansion, disturbing a ghostly piano, and sit outside smoking as Gatsby refuses to flee, clinging to the illusion that Daisy will choose him, while revealing his past with Dan Cody and his desperate attempt to win her as a penniless officer. On the last afternoon before the narrator’s departure abroad, he shares a tender, wordless moment with Daisy in a firelit room, their physical gestures conveying a profound, fading love. Meanwhile, Wilson, consumed by grief and suspicion after Myrtle’s death, fixates on the yellow car and the dog-leash found on her bureau, declaring God sees everything as he stares at the eyes of Doctor T. J. Eckleburg. Wilson traces the car to Gatsby’s house, learning his name, while Gatsby, having stopped using his pool all summer, puts on his bathing suit and waits by the pool for a call that never comes, having lost faith in his dream. The narrator, unable to reach Gatsby by phone due to a long-distance call from Detroit, marks his train timetable before departing, only to return and find Gatsby’s body floating in the pool, killed by Wilson, whose corpse is later found in the grass — the tragic culmination of illusions shattered by reality.\\\"\\n270:        \\\"Wilson\\\",\\n289:      \\\"summary\\\": \\\"In the aftermath of Gatsby’s death, the narrator witnesses the grotesque media frenzy surrounding the tragedy, as police, photographers, and reporters swarm the mansion while false testimonies from Catherine and the detective’s label of Wilson as a 'madman' distort the truth. Desperate to honor Gatsby, the narrator attempts to contact Daisy and Meyer Wolfsheim—only to learn Daisy and Tom have fled without a trace, and Wolfsheim refuses to attend the funeral, citing 'important business' and denying any knowledge of Gatsby’s family. Gatsby’s father, Henry C. Gatz, arrives from Minnesota, visibly shaken, and reveals his pride in his son through a worn photograph of the mansion and a childhood book containing Gatsby’s meticulous self-improvement schedule. At the funeral, only Gatz, the Lutheran minister, servants, the postman, and the unexpected presence of the man with owl-eyed glasses attend, as rain falls and the grand house stands empty. The narrator later encounters Tom Buchanan, who admits he told Wilson Gatsby owned the car that killed Myrtle, justifying it as necessary, and the narrator, seeing Tom as a childlike figure, shakes his hand in pity before reflecting on the East as a haunting, distorted landscape. On his final night at Gatsby’s mansion, the narrator erases an obscene word scrawled on the steps, recalls the vanished music of the parties, and walks to the beach, where he contemplates Gatsby’s futile pursuit of the green light and the enduring human struggle against the current, pulled back into the past.\\\"\\n385:          \\\"statement\\\": \\\"Tom reacts aggressively when Mrs. Wilson mentions Daisy\\\",\\n386:          \\\"quote\\\": \\\"Tom Buchanan and Mrs. Wilson stood face to face discussing, in impassioned voices, whether Mrs. Wilson had any right to mention Daisy’s name\\\",\\n480:      \\\"canonical_name\\\": \\\"Wilson\\\",\\n482:        \\\"George B. Wilson\\\",\\n483:        \\\"George Wilson\\\",\\n485:        \\\"Myrtle Wilson\\\",\\n487:        \\\"Mrs. Wilson\\\"\\n495:          \\\"text\\\": \\\"George B. Wilson is a man of quiet dignity and deep suffering, whose life is marked by poverty, isolation, and the devastating loss of his wife. He endures emotional and physical deterioration after her death, culminating in a tragic, delusional conviction that he must avenge her, ultimately leading to his own demise. His character arc reveals a man broken by betrayal and despair, yet clinging to a fragile sense of moral clarity in his final moments.\\\",\\n504:          \\\"statement\\\": \\\"Wilson lives in extreme poverty and isolation\\\",\",\"is_error\":false}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"b290fa2a-4de7-452e-801d-91aeff2d1515\",\"uuid\":\"1b4efa5b-f584-4a6a-80e5-d24a30948be9\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"stdout\":\"46:        \\\"Myrtle Wilson\\\",\\n47:        \\\"George B. Wilson\\\",\\n53:      \\\"summary\\\": \\\"On a summer Sunday afternoon, Nick Carraway is forcibly pulled off a train stopped at the desolate Valley of Ashes by Tom Buchanan, where they meet Myrtle Wilson at her husband George B. Wilson’s run-down garage beneath the billboard of Doctor T. J. Eckleburg; Tom and Myrtle then travel by train to New York, with Myrtle riding in a separate car to avoid detection, before taking a lavender taxicab to her apartment on the top floor of a building at 158th Street, where she buys a dog, changes into a cream chiffon dress, and hosts a drunken gathering with her sister Catherine and her photographer neighbor Mr. McKee and his wife; as alcohol flows freely and Myrtle transforms from a vital, earthy woman into a performative, haughty figure, she boasts of aspirations including a massage, a dog collar, and a wreath for her mother’s grave, while Catherine reveals Myrtle married George believing he was a gentleman—only to bitterly regret it—un