words = [\"wilson\"]`:\n- `start_idx = 0` (no title)\n- `first = words[0] = \"wilson\"`\n- `last = words[-1] = \"wilson\"`\n- Returns `(\"wilson\", \"wilson\")`\n\nSo for validating \"Wilson\" + \"George Wilson\":\n- `first1 = \"wilson\"`, `last1 = \"wilson\"`\n- `first2 = \"george\"`, `last2 = \"wilson\"`\n- Line 1523: `if first1 and last1 and first2 and last2` → TRUE (all are non-empty)\n- Line 1525: `if last1 == last2 and first1 != first2` → \"wilson\" == \"wilson\" and \"wilson\" != \"george\" → TRUE\n- This should REJECT!\n\nSo the validation SHOULD be working! But it's not. This means either:\n1. The validation is not being called\n2. There's a bug in the validation logic that I'm missing\n3. The merge is happening elsewhere\n\nLet me check one more thing - maybe the issue is with how the names are normalized before validation. Let me look at what `words1` and `words2` actually are:\n\nLooking back at line 1469-1482:\n\n```python\n# Normalize names for comparison\ncanonical_lower = canonical.lower()\nalias_lower = alias.lower()\n\n# Remove special characters (keep letters, numbers, spaces)\ncanonical_clean = re.sub(r'[^\\w\\s]', '', canonical_lower)\nalias_clean = re.sub(r'[^\\w\\s]', '', alias_lower)\n\n# Split into words\nwords1 = canonical_clean.split()\nwords2 = alias_clean.split()\n\n# Filter out common title words\nsignificant1 = self._filter_title_words(words1)\nsignificant2 = self._filter_title_words(words2)\n```\n\nSo the words are split AFTER cleaning and lowercasing. This should be fine.\n\nActually, I wonder if there's another path where the merge is happening. Let me check if maybe the LLM suggested \"Myrtle Wilson\" as canonical with \"Wilson\" and \"George Wilson\" as aliases? Or some other combination?\n\nActually, I think the simplest thing to do is to just FIX the issue more directly. Instead of trying to debug why the current fix didn't work, let me implement a MORE ROBUST fix that catches this case earlier in the pipeline.\n\nLooking at the